Zhe University features students as the stars at its Academic Conference, showcasing research results that open minds - Xinhua News Agency
Using air to wash hands, breathing to diagnose diseases, and precise search for sources of smog. Last night, at the opening ceremony of Zhejiang University's 2016 Academic Year Conference, a series of groundbreaking academic achievements were recognized.
The main researchers behind these achievements were students, covering major fields such as environment, mechanical engineering, energy, medicine, life sciences, and humanities education. Students began with a curious idea and created many high-end inventions with practical applications, some of which have won awards domestically and internationally.
Biological sensors: Your home doctor for health checks
The team of ZHANG Diming, a doctoral student from the School of Biomedical Engineering, showcased a biosensor on-site that fits on half the size of a smartphone.
"You simply need to exhale gently, and this instrument can preliminarily detect your health condition based on the components in your exhaled gases."
Zhang Diming explains that this sensor connects to a smartphone, and after exhaling, a health report will display on the phone.
Smart watches that can detect health conditions usually monitor physical parameters such as temperature and pulse on the market.
And this biosensor leverages graphene, a novel material, with its target being chemical elements. Its keen "sense of smell" allows it to more deeply and accurately reflect one’s health condition.
Intelligent Operating Room Lights: Doctors' High-Energy Eyes
Besides smartphones, smart operating lights are also used in the operating room.
This invention won a silver award at the 44th International Exhibition of Inventions of Switzerland in Geneva this year.
The master's student team from the School of Optoelectronic Science and Engineering, Zhu Linjing and Shen Junfei, opened up new ideas in the shortcomings of traditional operating lamps and invented an intelligent LED operating shadowless lamp.
Many early disease detection and removal are inadequate because of the lights doctors use in the operating room.
Shen Junfei says that during early cancer screenings, if doctors cannot clearly see all tumor tissues in time, cancer cells will spread later on.
This intelligent surgical lamp has four light discs that adjust the spectrum to enhance the distinguishability of different tissues under illumination.
During surgery, doctors can adjust the size, brightness, spectrum, color uniformity, and shadowless rate of the light spot as needed and according to their personal preference at any time.
This intelligent operating lamp has been piloted in hospitals such as Zhejiang University School of Medicine Affiliated Shǒuyífu Hospital and Zhejiang University School of Medicine Affiliated Children's Hospital, receiving good feedback.
Last October, this invention won first place in the National Challenge Cup.
Washing hands with air: Saves water and is cool and trendy
Ordinary people wash their hands by rinsing them with water, at most rubbing some soap and squeezing some hand sanitizer, while Li Qizhang and his classmates invented an "air washing device."
Standing on the sink of the device, our weight can compress the air out.
It's like a pump.
The compressed air forms a fluid at the faucet and combines with water.
So, we can wash our hands now! ”
The color reaction and bacterial residue test prove that the effectiveness of washing hands this way is the same as that of regular handwashing methods.
This device relies solely on our own gravity as the driving force, ensuring cleaning efficiency while saving 90% of water. The entire process is completely energy-free and environmentally friendly.
Last year, this invention stood out among top universities around the world at the Second Global Challenges Summit and won the sole gold award.
