A certificate that feels very meaningful – an emergency rescue certificate
This is one of the important skill certificates I value beyond my driver's license!
To clarify: first aid actually refers to emergency rescue measures during emergencies, i.e., the rescue conducted before the arrival of an ambulance.
It is well known that there is actually a period of time during which an ambulance arrives after an incident, and it is precisely this period that is most critical.
This is when emergency care is needed.
If I actually have a heart attack, cerebral hemorrhage, or sudden respiratory failure, then our window of life would be four minutes, golden four minutes!
So knowing how to perform first aid is so important to those around you!
When someone we know falls down, we can rescue them through professional knowledge rather than standing there helplessly doing nothing. The feeling of helplessness is terribly scary.
In China, we have official data indicating that 300,000 people die from unintentional deaths each year, the most dangerous of which is cardiac arrest.
In the United States, on average, 15% survive.
In Las Vegas casinos, about 70-80% can be saved, while in Shanghai and Beijing, it is below 1%.
This is what First Reaction founder Lu Le said.
If someone suffers a cardiac arrest with no breathing and no heartbeat, pressing on the point between the nose and mouth is ineffective.
Only artificial respiration and external chest compressions can possibly save the person.
I've found that we still have very few people with this awareness to learn first aid, but it's too late when something happens, so I still urge everyone to learn this skill so that we can respond promptly in case of emergencies.
It might even save someone's life!
The emergency medical certificate can be enrolled at your local Red Cross training center; I enrolled at the Red Cross training center in Zhuhai, where students also get a discount.
PS: The last picture records the goals I wanted to achieve before turning 25. Adding the first-aid certificate to the list of goals is something that happened a few months ago, but I did complete it!
Next, I plan to go for other certifications I want to get, and I am thinking of getting a diving certification. I saw on Little Red Book that there is also a Diving First Aid certificate, and it seems you need a first aid certificate to take the diving certificate, so in fact, a first aid certificate is quite useful!
