To start with, in my opinion, "Four Sea" is a complete disaster, in various senses.
But to say that this movie has nothing remarkable about it would be incorrect, at least the soundtrack was well chosen.
Rotten films come in many varieties, some that make you fall asleep comfortably at the cinema and others that leave you incensed after watching them. "Four Sea" is like a monkey on Emei Mountain to viewers—it can set your anger aflame in countless ways.
In terms of insulting the audience, Han Ge is truly gifted.
What's most painful about this film is that it feels like a creation from a K-drama, indescribable, with as many flaws as Michael Kwan's jacket.
Even after watching it, you might wonder if there is an underlying metaphor from the director, but upon closer thought, you realize it’s just a pile of feces.
To start simply, there are some plot points that I find difficult to understand.
The main character should live on a small island in the southern part of the country, depending on his grandmother.
Her grandmother wished to see snow, and the plot arranged for an elderly man in the neighborhood to drive her in a small van to northeast to see it snow.
Do elderly people sit in a van from the south to northeast to see snow?
He and his father feel very reassured about this.
And then the old man was no more.
How did the main character's brother obtain a business license to run a hotel on an abandoned ship?
Why do the motorcycle gangs on the island manage to block off a mountain road so effortlessly?
Then the truck delivering rebar in the middle of the road stopped by the side of the road, and even the truck driver couldn't be found around.
The vibe of this is way too obvious.
How does a petty rickshaw gangster with a bad credit history and suspicious criminal behavior become an auxiliary police officer?
Why don't people report high-interest loans when faced with them? Why did the police in the movie even ask if there were any difficulties?
An adult who knows how to use a smartphone but doesn’t know how to use a room key to open the door in a hotel, and ends up forcing his way into a room without finding the hotel manager, sitting outside the door all night?
This inexplicable plot permeates the entire film, and its absurdity is so extreme that recalling it causes me pain.
Beyond the plot, even worse is the film’s message, or rather, the film itself has no clear message at all, being a repulsive patchwork creature.
At first, you think it's a comedy, but about 20 minutes in, you wonder if it might be an inspirational film. By halfway through, you think it may be a drama about how tough it is for town youth to survive. After watching it, you have no idea what you just saw, and this leads to deep self-doubt: why go to the cinema on New Year's Day to appreciate such excrement.
For most people, the plot of the film is very familiar, for example, if you want to shoot a motorcyclist, it's a motivational+comedy and then you tell the audience how a talented bird moves step by step towards success.
If you want to make a love story, then just openly talk about the conflicts between the townsfolk who like riding motorcycles and the waitress girl.
If you want to reflect reality, at the very least your plot should not feature things that contradict the normal world.
A role like the one played by Yin Zheng, where a new motorcycle he just got burned is still able to sing, cannot go into some ridiculous imaginings even Kennedy would find hard to stomach.
As for the female lead, who is clearly not dumb but behaves like a 5-year-old in certain aspects, I'm not sure if it's due to her acting or the director's requirements.
One thing I still don't understand is why Han Han, as a race car driver, is so enthusiastic about adding crash scenes in his own films?
Is this kind of film arranged in the Spring Festival archives to add to the crowds?
