South African doctor: While Omicron is highly transmissible, it tends to cause mild symptoms.
Many doctors and medical experts in South Africa believe that the symptoms caused by the Omicron variant of COVID-19 are less severe than those caused by the Delta variant, although it spreads faster.
According to an Associated Press report, as the Omicron variant spreads in South Africa, a physicians' organization comprising more than 5,000 doctors from Gauteng province reported that patients they are currently treating for COVID-19 are experiencing relatively mild symptoms, such as body aches and coughing, which are similar to those of the flu.
Dr. Unben Pillay, the director of the organization, says he sees dozens of patients every day, but none require hospitalization.
Peele said: "They can control the disease at home, with most people recovering within 10 to 14 days of isolation."
He said it includes elderly patients and those with health issues, groups that are typically more vulnerable to becoming critically ill from COVID-19 infections.
He says that compared to the previous wave of the Delta outbreak, the COVID patients he treated were “short of breath and had low oxygen levels.”
Many need to be hospitalized in a few days.
Within two weeks of the first report of the Omicron variant in southern Africa, other doctors shared similar experiences.
While it will still take several weeks to collect enough data, doctors' observations and early evidence provide some clues.
According to the National Institute for Communicable Diseases in South Africa: In the recent weeks, only 30% of hospitalized COVID patients have severe cases, half the proportion from the previous wave; the average hospital stay has been reduced from 8 days to 2.8 days; and recently infected patients have a death rate of just 3%, compared to a 20% fatality rate in early cases in South Africa.
The head of Africa Health Research Institute, Willem Hanekom, cited data from the National Institute of Communicable Diseases and other reports to say: "Currently, almost all the indications point to this being a milder disease."
He also said, "It is still early, and we need to obtain later data."
Usually, the onset of hospitalization and death occurs later, while we entered this wave of the pandemic only two weeks ago.”
Meanwhile, scientists around the world are observing case numbers and hospitalization rates while testing the efficacy of current vaccines and treatments.
While Delta remains the predominant coronavirus strain worldwide, Omicron cases are now appearing in dozens of countries, with South Africa at the epicenter of the outbreak.
According to health officials, the number of new cases detected in the first week of December in Gauteng has increased by 400%, with tests showing that over 90% of cases are attributed to the Omicron variant.
