Education Comment: What Is Needed to Save Kidnapped Children?
If we were to compare it to the famous anti-trafficking slogan "No Purchase, No Killing," in an environment where there is no demand for "reclaiming abducted children," through mass prevention and control and public anti-trafficking efforts, making trafficking activities unprofitable, risky, and costly, such crimes would not occur frequently.
Recently, the Missing Children Emergency Response Platform developed by the Criminal Investigation Bureau of the Ministry of Public Security went online.
This is an internet platform created to establish an authoritative channel for missing children information releases and promptly recover abducted children.
Hearing that the app is called “ reunification, ” a sense of warmth instantly comes over me.
New technological means are not only named "cultural" and full of sentiment, but are also appropriately applied informatively, practical, and effective.
Anti-trafficking law enforcement's primary goal is to recover abducted children and protect their safety and freedom.
And then comes the trial and punishment of suspects.
The significance of criminal punishment ranges from individual punishment, social prevention, to legal education, and many "big words" could be cited.
However, priority should be given to the safety of the victims before trafficking cases are solved.
Issues should be prioritized based on urgency. Topics like whether kidnapping children should always be punishable by death, which are controversial and widely discussed, can be put on hold for careful discussion; however, rescuing abducted children cannot wait.
Based on experience with such cases, traffickers tend to flee the crime scene as quickly as possible after committing the act.
Every minute a search operation delays puts the child one step farther from their parents, increasing the difficulty of the case investigation by one degree.
In this case, coming up with effective solutions like the “Couple Reunion” platform, which directly helps in solving cases and recovering children, is indeed pragmatic action.
After all, "cracking down on trafficking" is a big task that requires the entire legal system to be put into operation.
Combatting crime, safeguarding children and parents’ rights, from the improvement of legislation to judicial protection, and meticulous enforcement, all require the implementation of care for “protecting the young and the weak.”
Moreover, better prevention of child trafficking, from families to society, is still possible.
For instance, a common case is a family of three resting at the railway station, where the parents fall asleep and wake up to find the child missing.
One cannot expect a two-year-old child to have sufficient self-protection skills, but as a parent of a young child, one should at least have a corresponding level of protective awareness.
Alternating rest for both parents or placing children in safe custody are some of the most basic measures to prevent child abduction.
From the other side of the police crackdown on trafficking crimes, those who buy trafficked children should also be concerned about and punished.
A single illegal act is often just one link in a longer chain.
According to China's Criminal Law, the statutory minimum sentence for the crime of trafficking in children is 5 years; in cases of particularly serious circumstances, it may be as high as death penalty with confiscation of property.
With a high starting point and heavy maximum sentence, trafficking in children appears to be a crime with heavier penalties in an overall trend towards leniency.
There are still many cases of trafficking occurring every year.
It is evident that some people have outdated views on bringing up children and lack legal awareness.
Legislative education should be synchronized for this group.
To effectively control trafficking crimes, strict enforcement by the official authorities is not enough.
If trafficking “markets” exist widely and traffickers are greedy for profit, constantly innovating their methods of trafficking to form a “game” with law enforcement, then anti-trafficking efforts will inevitably fall into an inefficient quagmire.
Therefore, dispelling backward ideas and promoting legal knowledge can also help eradicate child trafficking and child buying crimes.
Yang Haodong preached the spirit of the 6th Plenary Session of the 19th CPC Central Committee and the provincial Party congress at Ma Lankan.
Draw inspiration for progress from the Party's century-long struggle history.
The Hunan Daily (December 10) – (Full-media reporter Gong Bawei) According to unified arrangements by the provincial Party committee, on the morning of December 10th, Cai Haodong, member of the Standing Committee of the Provincial Party Committee and Minister of Propaganda Department of the Provincial Party Committee, delivered an interactive lecture at Ma'an Mountain Video Cultural and Creative Park, deepening the study and implementation of the spirit of the Sixth Plenary Session of the 19th CPC Central Committee, President Xi Jinping's important speeches and instructions concerning Hunan, and the spirit of the 12th Provincial Party Congress.
Yang Haodong expounded on the core essence and practical requirements of the spirit of the meeting from three perspectives: ideals and beliefs as well as the mission and responsibility, self-revolution and the youthful party, and the leading figures and theories of the Party, drawing upon his own insights and personal experiences to provide an accessible and comprehensive explanation.
He pointed out that one should combine the spirit of the plenary session with Comrade Xi Jinping's important speeches and instructions on Hunan, the spirit of the provincial Party congress, and study them integrally to enhance the ideological, political, and action-oriented consciousness in safeguarding “two establishments” and upholding “two maintenances.” One should proceed unwaveringly along the direction indicated by Comrade Xi Jinping.
During the interactive session, representatives from grassroots cadres, enterprises, and young people asked about the relationship between the three historical resolutions, how to understand the decisive significance of "two establishments," how cultural enterprises should grasp the orientation, and how contemporary youth can draw strength from the century-old party history. Yang Haodong answered each question with simple language and vivid examples. He earnestly encouraged young party members to draw strength for progress from the century-long struggle history of the party, to closely unite around the Central Committee of the Party, to be unwavering in following the Party's lead, and to write responsibility and commitment through their youth.
The expected number of university graduates in Beijing region in 2022 is around 268,000 - Xinhua News Agency
Xinhua News Agency, Beijing, December 11 (Xinhua reporter Yang Na) From the recent video conference jointly held by the Beijing Municipal Education Commission and the Municipal Human Resources and Social Security Bureau to discuss 2022 college graduate employment and entrepreneurship work, it was learned that the expected number of graduates from Beijing's universities this year is around 268,000, an increase of 16,000 compared to last year.
A relevant official from the Beijing Municipal Human Resources and Social Security Bureau stated that employment work for graduates from Beijing universities would be deployed through measures such as strengthening supply-demand matching, reinforcing policy support, broadening job-seeking channels, ensuring assistance to those in difficulty, supporting entrepreneurship and innovation, and improving service efficiency.
The Beijing Municipal Human Resources and Social Security Bureau is building practice platforms to increase job supply, actively contacting 40 university graduate workplace experience bases, visiting employers, and integrating demand and job information; focusing on "six batches," which include expanding recruitment for public institutions, expanding recruitment for state-owned enterprises, expanding recruitment for grassroots projects, expanding enlistment for the military, expanding placement of internships to retain graduates, and expanding entrepreneurship to drive employment; implementing "one-to-one" dynamic management, recommending no less than five job information options to each graduate, ensuring that all graduates from difficult families with employment aspirations are fully supported.
