The International Olive Oil Council classifies olive oil into two main categories: Virgin Olive Oil and Lampante Olive Oil (or Refined Olive Oil), with five levels.
Pressed olive oil, also known as virgin olive oil, is obtained from fresh olive fruits through mechanical cold pressing and filtration to remove impurities.
They are divided into three levels based on acidity:
Extra Virgin Olive Oil: The highest grade and best quality olive oil, which is a pure natural product.
It has exquisite flavors with a delicate aroma of plants, and acidity does not exceed 1%.
Fine Virgin Olive Oil: Slightly higher in acidity but not more than 2%, with a pure and aromatic flavor.
Ordinary Virgin Olive Oil: With acceptable taste and flavor, with acidity not exceeding 3.3%.
Refined olive oil is olive oil obtained from the refining of virgin olive oil with an acidity exceeding 3.3%, or "second oil."
Refined olive oil is divided into two grades:
Extra virgin olive oil (Olive Oil): A blend of refined olive oil with a certain proportion of virgin olive oil to harmonize flavors and colors, with an acid content of less than 1.5%, and a transparent pale golden hue.
Refined Olive-Pomace Oil (also known as Olive-Pomace Oil): This is olive oil extracted and refined from the pomace through dissolution methods.
The acidity of olive oil, which you should have noticed from reading about grading categories, is a very important indicator.
To know, like grapes used for wine-making, olives from different regions and varieties of olives will produce olive oil of different standards.
The Best Place for Oil Olive Growth:
The Mediterranean coast has the most suitable climate conditions for olive-growing, including soil and temperature, sunlight, humidity of the air, and rainfall.
Outside of the Mediterranean coast, other regions can only be sparsely planted in their suitable zones.
Major producing regions (Mediterranean):
Italy, Spain, Greece, Turkey, Portugal, Morocco, Syria, Israel, Tunisia, Algeria, Egypt, Pakistan, Lebanon, among others, have climates similar to those in countries like Argentina and Peru in southern latitudes, as well as South Africa, etc.
Harvesting Month for Olive Oil:
From March to June each year, it is the time when olive trees flower and bear fruit; from July to October, the fruits begin to develop and ripen, turning from green to red, purple, and finally black.
November to February of the following year is the season for harvesting fruits (the freshness of olive oil is indeed important).
Nutritional components of olive oil:
Olive oil is rich in monounsaturated fatty acids such as oleic acid and linoleic acid, as well as vitamins A, B, D, E, K, and antioxidants.
Olive oil is considered to be the most suitable oil for human nutrition among the oils discovered to date.
The Democracy Summit concludes, and Biden says the flame of freedom will never be extinguished.
The online "Summit for Democracy," convened by US President Biden and attended by government leaders from 110 countries, civil society representatives, and private sector leaders, concluded on Friday (December 10).
Biden said in his closing remarks that the flame of freedom burns in the hearts of people around the world, and authoritarian regimes can never extinguish it.
He also said that he plans to hold a second Global 'Democracy Summit' next year.
Taiwan’s government representative and Information Minister Teng Fong, as well as Lo Guanhong, a pro-democracy and human-rights activist currently in exile in the UK, also spoke at the summit on the second day.
Biden said: "We know how daunting the work before us is."
But we also know that we can meet the challenge, as I have said before, just as this summit shows, democracy is everywhere.
Authoritarian regimes can never put out the flicker of freedom that burns in the hearts of people all around the world and everywhere.
Biden also stated that defending democracy requires the concerted effort of society as a whole.
He said, "As leaders of the government, we have a responsibility to listen to our citizens, fortify the bulwark of democracy, push for reforms, achieve transparent accountability governance, be better able to resist the buffer and shock of authoritarianism, and those who want to pursue power nakedly before public interest."
The second day of the "Democracy Summit" focuses on defending human rights, consolidating democracy, countering authoritarianism, and countering digital authoritarianism while reaffirming democratic values.
It fell on International Human Rights Day.
UN Secretary-General António Guterres also noted in his opening remarks that the cause of democracy faces challenges globally, particularly from emerging digital technologies.
He said: "It is now time to reaffirm our shared values and positions, as well as the resilience of democracy."
This requires deepening societal dialogue, addressing inequality, fighting corruption, updating social contracts, investing in quality education, and expanding opportunities for women and girls.
This also requires firm condemnation of all forms of populism, nationalism, and extremism, including all tools provided by the technological revolution. ”
Hong Kong democracy and human rights activist Lo Guolong delivered his remarks via a pre-recorded video at the summit, reminding participants to continue paying attention to human rights conditions in Hong Kong and among China’s minorities, such as the Uighurs and Tibetans.
He said: "For a long time, the world has accepted China's rise without establishing a mechanism to hold it accountable."
That is why political figures and civil society leaders must cooperate.
We must use everything we have (resources) to ensure that the revival of democracy is our top priority.”
Lu Guangcong also calls for unity among the free world to counteract the Chinese Communist Party's threats.
He said: "Perhaps some of you are afraid of angering General Secretary Xi Jinping, perhaps some of you do not want to lose the Chinese market, or perhaps some of you have not recognized (the Communist Party's) threat to our democratic values."
And that is why we failed.
This is also why we must change.”
Taiwan’s Minister of Administration Deputy Chen Fenggong, attending a roundtable discussion titled "Fighting Digital Authoritarianism and Affirming Democratic Values," released the “State Statement” via video, emphasizing Taiwan’s stance at the forefront in combating autocracy and authoritarianism.
He also shared his experience of fighting the pandemic through information transparency and building mutual trust.
He said that, while democracy in Taiwan is on the rise, we have always stood resolutely at the forefront of global efforts to counter authoritarianism, and we have also played a leading role in promoting global freedom, democracy, and human rights.
During the pandemic, authoritarian governments in various places justified human rights violations under the guise of public health and collective interest, indicating a retreat of global democracy.
However, in Taiwan, we can control the pandemic without citywide lockdowns and defeat misinformation without removing it from shelves.
While the agenda for the "Democracy Summit" was underway on its second day, the U.S. Treasury Department announced sanctions against 15 individuals and 10 entities from countries such as China and North Korea, which it deemed to have seriously violated human rights, and imposed investment restrictions on a Chinese company that was found to be using surveillance technology to assist in human rights abuses.
