Want to give tennis a try but afraid of not being able to stick with it, so want to find something around that price to practice first.
Unfriendly, in fact, price is often inversely proportional to friendliness. If one cannot distinguish, more expensive rackets are generally more friendly than cheaper ones, and these so-called beginner rackets are actually industrial waste.
They have very low impact strength; when a ball hits the paddle, it vibrates greatly. Hard balls cannot withstand this, and even skilled players find them difficult to control.
Their logic and underlying message is – since tennis is so difficult, they know you won’t be able to learn, they know you don’t want to spend a lot of money, so they throw you something cheap to fool you. After two times, you won’t play anymore, so the racket is cheap, and you won’t feel bad about it.
When you can truly distinguish between good and bad, it means you've progressed; then come buy the more expensive items from me, and I'll make twice the money.
Spend a similar price on a better second-hand racket, choose brands like Prince or Dunlop; these brands aren't very well-known, so the good rackets don’t sell at a high price. A new one costs around 500-600, while a second-hand one is around 200, isn't enjoying a thousand-dollar racket enjoyment more satisfying?
Friendly to beginners, but becomes useless after passing through the beginner stage, but most people, especially girls, cannot make it past the beginner stage.
