Xinhua Media Plus | Beijing Youth Ice Hockey League Fuels Olympic Fever - Xinhua News Agency
On December 11, Beijing Daring ING player Pang Yutian (center) shoots during a match.
On the day, the Beijing Youth Ice Hockey Club League of the 2021-2022 season continued to be contested at various ice rinks in Beijing.
This league consists of a regular season and a playoff, with athletes aged U6 to U18 participating. There are two groups: a competitive group and a selection group, attracting nearly 3,600 players from about 256 teams across the city.
The Beijing Youth Ice Hockey Club League, which has developed over more than a decade, has now become a platform for Beijing's youth ice hockey enthusiasts to hone their skills and make new friends. It also provides top players for Beijing's youth teams and youth national teams.
The league's events have an impact on Beijing's youth ice hockey, igniting enthusiasm for Olympic ice hockey and laying the foundation for the development of ice hockey in Beijing after the Winter Olympics.
Girl Donates for Older Sibling as Target for Archers: "It's Fine to Think It’s Only Skin Wound" - Xinhuanet
A girl in Hangzhou's Longjing Square stands in front of a large wooden board that reads “Human Meat Target, 10 yuan per arrow.”
This morning, a photo of a "Hangzhou girl who volunteered as a 'target' to raise money for her sister" went viral on social media.
The photo shows a girl in Hangzhou's Longjing Square standing in front of a large wooden board that is slightly taller than a person, with the words "Human Meat Target, 10 yuan per arrow" written on it, indicating that passersby can use bows and arrows after paying.
The behavior sparked online discussion.
Last night, a spokesperson for the Public Security Bureau of Shucheng District, Hangzhou City, confirmed to Beijing News reporters that the police had verified that the girl had made such an extreme move in order to raise funds for her sister, but this behavior is not worth promoting. The girl left the local police station yesterday after being warned.
Girl Street "Target" for Fundraising Warned
In the posted picture, a long-haired girl stands in a “person” shape in front of a large wooden board, with black ink writing "Human Meat Target, Ten Yuan per Arrow" on it. Another wooden board in front of her writes down her bank account and WeChat and Alipay account numbers.
The image caused controversy after it was posted online.
Some netizens view this as "attention-seeking beggary," while many others sympathize with it.
That is what we observed around 10 AM on the 23rd when we were patrolling near the square; at that point, there were already quite a number of onlookers.
The police officers from the TianShui police station near the incident site, when interviewed by local media, said that there were two young girls selling goods, both around 20 years old, who were dressed in youthful and attractive outfits.
One of the girls who posed as “human targets” was crying and begging passersby to play the game; another girl stood nearby cheering her up, saying that her friend’s sister had leukemia and urgently needed surgery funds; please help.
“We contacted local police in Guizhou and learned that the girl was fundraising for her sister’s leukemia treatment.”
Last night, a publicity officer from Hangzhou Xiaocheng Public Security Bureau told a reporter for Beijing News that the girl's name is Ji Jiari and she is fundraising for her sister Ji Jiayan.
But since the treatment still requires over 300,000 in fees, no good way to raise funds was thought of, and this behavior was done as a result.
He said that during the entire process, one of the two girls acted as a target while the other assisted without being involved in shooting; however, no arrows were fired throughout the process, and it was just people gathering to take photos which were subsequently posted on Weibo.
Subsequently, the police determined through an investigation department's inspection that the bows and arrows used were not within the controlled category.
The responsible person said, "Such attention-grabbing fundraising methods are not advocated."
She has left the police station after being warned by a police officer.
