VNA - GCC Responds to Common Challenges
Saudi Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman's stopovers include Oman, the United Arab Emirates (UAE), Qatar, Bahrain, and Kuwait.
Notably, this is the first visit by Crown Prince Mohammed since Saudi Arabia and its Arab allies imposed an embargo on Qatar in mid-2017, leading to a Gulf diplomatic crisis.
In this crisis, Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates, Bahrain, and Egypt severed diplomatic relations with Qatar and imposed a full embargo on it, accusing Qatar of supporting terrorist organizations while having close ties to Iran.
The diplomatic tensions have severely affected the internal cohesion of the Gulf Cooperation Council and had negative effects on the economies of the countries in the region.
Four Arab countries have blocked land, sea, and air routes to Qatar.
This crisis was only resolved at the Gulf Cooperation Council summit in January 2021, when the Gulf countries signed an agreement mediated by the United States and Kuwait towards "unity and stability."
Saudi Arabia has reopened its borders with Qatar.
Saudi Arabia and Egypt are leading efforts to restore relations with Qatar by appointing new ambassadors between the two countries in Qatar.
The Gulf Arab states’ efforts to resolve their differences and restore relations with Qatar stem from the necessity to unite in order to serve the interests of all countries and regional stability, in order to address common challenges.
During the visit of the Saudi Crown Prince to Oman, both sides agreed to open the first direct land route between two neighboring Gulf countries.
The 725-kilometer-long corridor connecting the two countries will facilitate travel and supply chain integration for citizens of both nations.
The Public Investment Fund of Saudi Arabia (PIF) will invest $5 billion in Oman, where 13 Memoranda of Understanding worth $30 billion were signed by the enterprises of the two countries.
The visit of Saudi Crown Prince to the Gulf countries coincides with negotiations between Iran and major world powers over the Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action (JCPOA) – an Iranian nuclear agreement that the Gulf Arab states have condemned for being ineffective in resolving their missile issue.
Each Gulf state has viewed Iran as a long-term "competitor" and seen Iran's nuclear program as a "threat" to regional security.
All Gulf states argue that failing to address Iran’s missile issue and taking action to curb Iran’s activities in the region is flawed.
Against the backdrop of failed talks between major countries and Iran over its nuclear program that did not meet the hopes of the Gulf states, Saudi Arabia and Iran held direct negotiations.
Its goal is to contain further escalation of tensions in the region.
This is one of the factors that slows down economic growth in Gulf Cooperation Council countries.
The upcoming GCC summit will be the first GCC meeting since some of its member states ended their dispute with Qatar.
This is an opportunity for the Gulf Cooperation Council to reassess its diplomatic achievements in promoting the restoration of GCC unity, while also proposing measures to ensure full cooperation among its members to address common challenges and contribute to the stability and development of the Gulf countries.
