The maximum duration of suspension for college students in Guangxi to start their own businesses is up to 5 years - Xinhua News Agency
Xinhua News Agency, Nanning, May 19, 2023 (Reporter Zhang Ying) – Recently, from the Education Department of the Guangxi Zhuang Autonomous Region, it was learned that in the future, universities across Guangxi will implement flexible academic systems, relaxing the duration of students' leaves of absence. The period for students to leave school to start their own businesses can be extended by 2 to 5 years beyond the original curriculum duration, and the time spent on entrepreneurship can be regarded as practical education time.
To promote the enhancement of college students’ entrepreneurial innovation capabilities, relevant departments such as education and human resources in Guangxi have cooperated to build entrepreneurship platforms, establishing more off-campus practice teaching bases for college students, university technology parks, college student startup zones, and entrepreneurship incubators, thereby providing external guarantees for college students’ entrepreneurship.
According to the requirements, by 2020, the area of the entrepreneurship and innovation practice bases for universities should be above 3,000 square meters for undergraduate schools, and above 2,000 square meters for vocational colleges.
The government encourages universities to establish entrepreneurial and innovative credit points and prioritizes supporting students who excel in their entrepreneurial and innovative practices to switch majors.
College teachers should spend at least two months in industry enterprises each two years to enhance their awareness and ability in teacher entrepreneurship and innovation education.
The Education Department of the Autonomous Region requires universities in Guangxi to implement the College Students Innovation and Entrepreneurship Training Program in depth, aiming for each college student to participate in at least one innovation and entrepreneurship training program during their time at school.
Colleges and universities should actively organize students to participate in various levels of innovation and entrepreneurship competitions and provide funding support.
NEWS GOV-MO: Australia Honors Seven Outstanding Individuals with Doctor of Laws Degrees
The University of Macau held today an “Honorary Doctorates and Higher Degrees Conferral Ceremony 2021,” conferring Honorary Doctorates from the University of Macau to Liu Zhenyu, Chen Kaixian, Hiroshi Nakamura, Li Lanjuan, Li Xialiang, Zhu Liran, and Jiang Chaohui in recognition of their outstanding achievements and significant contributions to society and education.
At the conferral ceremony, on behalf of the Chief Executive and the Chair of the Board of Governors of the University, Secretary for Social and Cultural Affairs Ouyang Yu conferred the Doctor of Honor degree at the University with the assistance of Chairman Lin Jincheng of the University’s Board of Governors and President Song Yonghua of the University.
Professor Liu Zhenyu was awarded an honorary Doctor of Education degree, Professor Chen Kaixian was awarded an honorary Doctor of Science degree, Dr. Nakamura Hiroshi was awarded an honorary Doctor of Science degree, Professor Li Lanjuan was awarded an honorary Doctor of Science degree, Professor Li Xiaoliang was awarded an honorary Doctor of Business Administration degree, Professor Zhu Lilan was awarded an honorary Doctor of Science degree, and Ms. Jiang Chaohui was awarded an honorary Doctor of Science degree.
During his address, Ouyang Yu expressed warm congratulations and high respect to the seven distinguished individuals who were awarded Honorary Doctorates for their remarkable achievements across various fields, which have made outstanding contributions to the development of the country and the advancement of the world; he also conveyed his most heartfelt blessings to the graduates and parents, and paid sincere respects to the dedicated and scholarly faculty team of the university.
Professor Sun Yifeng, who read Professor Liu Zhongyu’s eulogy at the Australian National University, noted that Professor Liu is a globally renowned economist known for his outstanding academic achievements and forward-thinking educational philosophy.
In addition to his achievements in economic development, economic growth, and East Asia economics, he is also a visionary education leader.
In essence, he has made notable contributions in numerous fields.
Sun Yifeng read out Professor Nakamura Shuji's eulogy, stating that Professor Nakamura is a pioneer of sustainable energy technology.
He became one of the three winners of the 2014 Nobel Prize in Physics for inventing the blue light-emitting diode (LED).
Professor Nakamura's invention not only revolutionized the lighting industry but also gave rise to today's widespread white light-emitting diodes (LEDs) used in daily life and industrial production, making significant contributions to human welfare.
Sun Yifeng read out the eulogy for Li Lanjuan, expressing that Professor Li was an pioneer of China's artificial liver program and established a unique and effective Li’s artificial liver system, achieving major breakthroughs in treating severe hepatitis and hepatic failure, significantly reducing the mortality rate of hepatic failure.
In the fight against COVID-19, her team's proposed "Four Antevers and Two Balances" solution, along with the technologies of artificial liver, microbiome, and stem cells they brought, significantly improved the mortality rate for severe cases.
Sun Yifeng read out the eulogy for Professor Li Xialiang, stating that Professor Li laid the foundation for innovative research in operations management, shaped the thinking behind supply chain theory and practice, and fundamentally transformed business models worldwide.
Li Xiaoliang is known for popularizing the concept of the Bullwhip Effect in supply chain management and exploring measures to address this phenomenon.
Now, with the pandemic and economic tensions, the stick effect is becoming more pronounced, leading to severe disruptions in supply chains.
Li Xialiang's visionary research serves as a ray of hope, helping the industry to navigate through tough times and bring about significant social value.
Sun Yifeng read out the eulogy for Zhu Liang, stating that in her research work, she discovered a breakthrough and innovative staining technique used to prepare samples, which enjoys high reputation among domestic and international scientific peers.
She was awarded important honorary titles such as Fellow of the International Academy of European Sciences for her remarkable technological achievements, thus securing her rightful lofty status in the temple of world science.
Dr. Chen Xin, on behalf of the Executive President of the Australian Chinese Medicine Research Institute, read out Professor Chen KaiXian's tribute, stating that Professor Chen has made distinguished scientific achievements throughout more than four decades in drug research and development, and his significant contributions to the country, Macao, the University of Macao, and National Key Laboratories have earned him a high international standing and reputation.
His proactive role in promoting the development of traditional Chinese medicine in Macau and the Greater Bay Area fully embodies his sense of mission to contribute to the country's pharmaceutical strategy.
President of Macao Science and Technology College Cai Chengzhong read the tribute for Jiang Chaohui, stating that the significant inventions and patents developed by Ms. Jiang have accumulated over 15 items related to storage virtualization, navigation, file systems, and power management, all of which are highly practical, widely used, and enhance human convenience in daily life.
As an accomplished inventor, Ms. Jiang is often able to simplify complex technology and theories in a way that makes them accessible to the general public.
Lastly, Chen KaiXian read the farewell remarks on behalf of the honorary doctoral representatives.
He was pleased to see that pharmaceuticals in Macao and the Greater Bay Area have set a good example: from the first National Key Laboratory of Traditional Chinese Medicine Quality Research established at the University of Macau to the thriving development of TCM in the Greater Bay Area, the University of Macau has made significant contributions in integrating top talents in the TCM field across the Greater Bay Area.
Furthermore, he believes that the management team at Macquarie University has foresight, not only providing fertile ground for academic research for students but also producing many responsible talents that benefit society.
It was a great honor for him to engage in exchanges and cooperation with AUT, and he sincerely congratulated AUT on its recent achievements.
