Asian American groups complain of Ivy League schools like Yale discriminating in admissions - Xinhua News Agency
Xinhua News Agency, Washington, May 23 (Xu Jianmei) – On May 23, more than 130 Asian-American groups submitted an appeal to the U.S. Department of Education and the Justice Department protesting against the discrimination against Asian-American applicants in the admissions process of three Ivy League universities – Yale University, Brown University, and Dartmouth College.
The Asian American Education Coalition, which spearheaded the appeal, held a news conference in Washington that day.
Chairman Zhao Yuke stated at the press conference that over the past ten years, the number of Asian applicants meeting the requirements for admission to top American universities has grown exponentially; however, in these three prestigious American universities, the proportion of Asians remained largely at the same level.
Many studies show that these colleges engage in what is euphemistically referred to as "holistic review," systematically and persistently discriminating against Asian American applicants.
Zhaoyukong explains that in 2014, scholars such as Sanders showed that among these three universities, Asians had the lowest acceptance rate relative to the number of applicants among all ethnic groups.
Data from the US Department of Education also shows that among all Ivy League schools, Brown University and Dartmouth College have the lowest acceptance rates for Asian students, and Yale University and Brown University have implemented de facto racial quotas for Asian applicants for over 20 years.
Zha Yukuang told a reporter with Xinhua that they expect the US Department of Education and Justice to launch an investigation after their appeal is submitted, and take action to prevent these universities from continuing to discriminate against Asians in admissions once facts are determined.
They also expect complaint universities to actively take this issue seriously and change their admissions policies towards Asians.
Currently, the three universities being complained about have not responded.
The Asian American Education Alliance was founded in 2015, when it had filed a complaint against Harvard University's admissions for discrimination, but the U.S. Department of Education declined to accept the case on procedural grounds.
In addition to Chinese-American groups, this time, Asian-American groups including Indian-American, Korean-American, Japanese-American, and Pakistani-American educational organizations have also participated in the appeals.
