In September 2020, Mr. Lu learned about a factory located in Zhongting Town, Yuyao, Zhejiang Province, from a real estate agent. After multiple site visits, Mr. Lu decided to purchase the factory.
In December of the same year, Mr. Lu had discussions with Mrs. Lin, the family of the property owner, regarding the details of buying the factory, and transferred 200,000 yuan as a deposit via bank transfer that same day.
Lin signed as the recipient on the earnest money receipt for the factory sale, which states, "If the buyer does not want to buy this factory, the earnest money will go to the seller. If the seller is unwilling to sell this factory, the earnest money will be doubled and returned to the seller. The parties have agreed that the factory sales contract will be signed within one week."
However, they were unable to reach an agreement on the main contract terms such as the scope of the factory sale and the payment method, which delayed the signing of the house purchase contract.
Considering that both parties had failed to reach consensus on the contract terms, Liu returned the 200,000 yuan deposit to Mr. Lu a few days later.
The house he had finally set his sights on, the deposit was already paid and the down payment was also ready, they just needed to sign the contract when the seller suddenly decided not to sell anymore. This made Mr. Lu both frustrated and angry.
Therefore, Mr. Lu filed a lawsuit against Lin in Yuyao People's Court.
The court, after reviewing the case, found that the parties ultimately failed to enter into a formal contract and this cannot solely be attributed to the defendant. It should reasonably be presumed that negotiations did not succeed, hence this case should not apply the penalty for a breach of the earnest money deposit. Therefore, the defendant is not required to bear the liability of double repayment of the earnest money deposit.
After the first-instance judgment, the plaintiff appealed.
Recently, the appellate court upheld the original judgment.
Lawyers advise to exercise necessary due diligence during the transaction process.
At the time of signing the contract, as the buyer, one must exercise necessary due diligence, such as verifying information about the property owner, whether the house is occupied, and stipulating in the contract the specific timing and location for delivery to avoid disputes during performance.
After the "Summit for Democracy" ended on Friday, China's Foreign Ministry spokesman made a statement on Saturday (December 11th) expressing that the US hosting the "Summit for Democracy" was "to draw ideological lines, to instrumentalize and militarize democratic tools under the guise of democracy, to carry out anti-democratic practices in the name of democracy, and to stir up division and confrontation while diverting domestic contradictions."
The Chinese government views that “for a long time, the US has imposed its political system and values on others, promoting 'democracy transformation', abusing unilateral sanctions, and instigating 'color revolutions', with catastrophic consequences.”
'Democracy' has already become America's 'weapon of mass destruction' in interfering with other countries.
This summit is an action taken by Biden to fulfill his campaign promises.
The two-day meeting was conducted via video, with leaders from 111 countries invited to attend, including Taiwan's leader, but China and Russia were not invited.
Ahead of the summit, China and Russia criticized the US for its own democratic issues, saying the US did not have the right to host such a summit.
And there are also doubts internationally and within the United States, such as criticism of leaders with authoritarian tendencies, including some heads of state from countries like the Philippines, Poland, and Brazil, being invited to attend. Some doubt that the Biden administration’s true intention was not to promote democracy but to organize the summit for geostrategic purposes.
Germany's newly elected Chancellor Scholz also attended the Video Democracy Summit.
The statement from the Chinese Foreign Ministry on Saturday noted, "Today's American democratic malaise is clearly evident: issues such as money politics, identity politics, party polarization, political extremism, social fragmentation, racial tensions, and wealth disparity continue to escalate," stating that American democracy is a "game for the rich" based on capital.
U.S. President Biden said in his remarks, “As leaders of the government, we have a responsibility to listen to our citizens, fortify the bulwarks of democracy, push for reforms, achieve transparent accountability governance, be better able to resist the buffers and shocks of authoritarianism, and those who seek naked power before public interest.”
He emphasizes that democracy is not an entitlement, “We must keep refreshing it from generation to generation, in my view, this is the decisive challenge of our time.”
Biden also said that “democracy and human rights face sustained and troubling challenges, and democracy needs champions to defend it.”
The US pledged to launch a year-long initiative following the Democracy Summit, which includes a $424.4 million initiative aimed at strengthening democracies worldwide and supporting independent media outlets.
The US hopes that countries will make specific public commitments before an upcoming physical Democracy Summit slated for late 2022, in order to improve their democratic systems.
Biden said, “Let us all implement our commitments and report on our progress next year.”
