Sabah 3,200 newborn babies fail to register due to shortage of custom anti-counterfeiting paper.
(SABAH, 11 October. The National Registration Bureau said that due to the depletion of stock of specially produced anti-counterfeiting paper, birth certificates could no longer be issued since early October. As a result, so far, about 3,200 affected people have accumulated.)
However, Director Kairu Aminu assured that birth certificates for all babies who have already applied would be issued once the counterfeit-proof papers arrive next week, with their information already recorded in the system.
At a press conference, he addressed a report from a web media outlet alleging issues with Sabah Report's paper printing that had been ongoing for over ten months.
He says that the problem arose two months ago, and Sarawak is facing the same issue.
"One reason is that manufacturers in China producing customized security paper have been delayed in shipping due to the impact of the pandemic."
He explained that the anti-counterfeiting paper used for Sabah birth certificates is different from that used in other regions, hence it cannot be borrowed from stock in other states.
Kaluaminus revealed that the latest batch of anti-counterfeit paper arrived in Kuala Lumpur on the 6th of this month and was handed over to the printing company on the 8th for some printing work.
The bureau will pick up the goods in Kuala Lumpur next Thursday and proceed with the final stage of printing upon return to Sabah, estimating that it can be handed over to the applicant two additional working days from now (December 20).
"We will notify the applicants to collect the birth certificate at the local branches."
He adds that Sabah has so far issued 24,801 birth certificates this year.
