I came across an article that discusses, from both a commercial and a shelf-life perspective, why this customer wants a logistics company in the bonded area to do bottling. Although bottling is considered a form of further processing and does not fall under the scope of operations for bonded logistics companies, perhaps this piece of text can explain why the customer wants to proceed this way?
I visited many milk powder manufacturers and interacted with many milk powder production lines.
In fact, the raw materials for powdered milk in pouches and tins are exactly the same, with no distinctions.
So people will inevitably ask: why is the shelf life of bagged food one year, while that of canned food can be two years or even longer?
Or one might ask: Why is there such a big price difference between canned and bagged products?
In fact, many consumers, or mothers, believe this is because the quality is much better due to the difference in raw materials.
Actually, it's the same raw material, but people just think that something expensive is good.
There are also consumers who eat it this way and then say that the taste or texture of bottled versions is different!
Now, let me first explain why bottled versions are more expensive, where the cost lies?
Why is there such a large price difference when their raw materials are the same?
Because manufacturers generally factor in that if the bag price is too high, consumers will find it hard to accept, this means that manufacturers make very low or no profits. So, how do manufacturers survive?
So there has been a significant increase in pricing for bottled goods to secure their profit margins.
Therefore, most manufacturers promote bottled versions rather than bagged ones.
And there are companies that spend tens of millions, or even hundreds of millions, on advertising every year. Where do they get all that money?
One can imagine.
So as society develops, marketing expenses such as promotional staff in malls, display costs in malls, product giveaways, and advertising fees are also increasing continuously.
Therefore, manufacturers are constantly adding new products, which are in 500ml cans, and these products are also becoming increasingly expensive. In fact, this is a new product that has emerged due to increased market fees.
The main purpose is to generate higher profits, and their additional cost for raw materials will not exceed about 3 or 4 yuan at most.
There are also brands that go to extreme lengths to promote their products, linking up with doctors at hospitals to assist in product promotion, earning commissions ranging from 30 to 80 yuan from each sale.
And such an approach is absolutely illegal under China's relevant laws, and in fact it is harming consumers.
It is recommended to buy powdered milk in bags and then pour it into cans at home if you already have cans, as this can save money while increasing the preservation and sealability of the milk, with no impact on children.
Also, do not think that the shelf life of bottled items is longer than that of bagged ones. The shelf life of both is the same after packaging is opened; it's just that bagged items are harder to preserve and more prone to getting damp and deteriorating after opening.
