Don't scare people away at the starting line - Xinhua News Agency
When browsing through book markets, one often feels the popularity of self-help books.
The current self-help books often promote the "best-case principle" – that is, assuming or implying that you can "do it," and then telling you what you need to do.
It means skipping over the most important and fundamental step of “how to do it.”
That is why all these books have one common characteristic – either go all out and do your best, or just don’t do anything at all; either 100% or nothing; either climb Mount Tai, or just stay put.
This "best principle," which everyone will acknowledge as "perfect" upon seeing it, would nonetheless leave 99% of people flat on their faces at the starting line.
Because this theory promotes a path of “you need to make a firm decision and start anew, with a 180-degree turnaround,” which is precisely what many people’s biggest weakness is.
For those who are conservative, resistant to change, and unwilling to adapt, shouting at them from their ear, "You need to change," is nothing but self-deception and hitting a cow with a piano.
Most people would falter at the word “transformation,” hesitating and lacking resolve; or they might make a hasty decision to embark on “transformation” out of impulse, bravely taking the first step, but it is often short-lived, as persistence does not last long, and they fail to sustain it for any considerable time.
Mr. Ma's renowned comedic sketch "From Tomorrow Onward" is the quintessential example of this "common humanity".
The biggest problem with the "Best Principle" is that it has a too high threshold, often exceeding the capability limits of ordinary people, leaving most to be "speechless in front of a grand entrance."
Perhaps, “second-best principle,” could be a route that allows many ordinary people to achieve relative success.
The core of the "Principle of Sufficiency" lies in "lowering the threshold"—it promotes a theory of "winning small battles to win big ones," which means that doing 1 is acceptable even if 100 cannot be achieved; for 1 is always greater than 0; and if one cannot reach Mount Tai, climbing to your front-yard dune is better than standing still.
In any case, doing something is always better than doing nothing.
With the threshold significantly lowered, there is more possibility for ordinary people to persevere, and the probability of success will also increase as long as one persists.
Following the "second-best" principle still leads to success, albeit on a longer path.
In essence, while the "best-fit principle" may tout success for everyone on the surface, it ultimately motivates only a few; in contrast, the "second-best principle" effectively motivates more people and aims to benefit as many as possible.
