The resource stock of Qinghai Lake huchen increased by 0.71 million tons this year - Xinhua.
Xinhua News Agency, Xining, Dec. 11 (Xu Xiaofei and Zhang Hongxiang)—The latest monitoring results show that the natural resources of naked carp in Lake Qinghai have increased by 0.71 million tons this year compared to last year, reaching a total of 10.85 million tons. Compared with the initial period of protection in 2002, the cumulative growth has exceeded forty times.
Arctic Grayling, also known as "Huang Yin" in Qinghai Lake, is a unique and precious fish species endemic to Qinghai Lake. It plays a crucial role in maintaining the ecological safety of the "water-fish-bird" chain in the lake and preserving biological diversity in the Qinghai Lake Basin.
In the 1960s and 1970s, due to excessive hunting by humans and deteriorating environmental conditions for the breeding of scaleless carp in Qinghai Lake, the population of scaleless carp in Qinghai Lake sharply declined. By 2002, the estimated biomass of scaleless carp resources in Qinghai Lake was only 2,592 tons.
In the Red List of Chinese Species in 2004, the Qinghai Lake Barbel was listed as an endangered species.
Since 2003, Qinghai Province has implemented a lake closure to breed fish, prohibiting any units, collectives, or individuals from fishing for huchen in Qinghai Lake and its main rivers and tributaries, as well as the sale of huchen and its products.
At the same time, by continuing to increase protection efforts through measures such as artificial seedstock release and cleaning of major rivers entering Qinghai Lake, as well as designing scientifically reasonable “fish passage facilities,” the stock of Qinghai Lake huchen has been increasing steadily.
Qianling, Deputy Director of the Agriculture and Rural Affairs Bureau of Qinghai Province, introduced that to date, over 180 million fry of Qinghai Lake huchen have been implemented in artificial spawning and release efforts, contributing more than 23% to the recovery of the Qinghai Lake huchen resource.
The Qinghai Three-River Source region is an important ecological barrier and water source conservation area not only for China but also for Asia, while Lake Qinghai serves as a natural barrier against desertification spreading eastward and an ecological functional zone.
To protect the ecological environment of the Three River Source region and Qinghai Lake waters, to protect aquatic wildlife, and to maintain biodiversity in the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau, since this year, Qinghai has successively issued notices banning fishing in key waters of the Yangtze River, Yellow River, and Qinghai Lake, establishing a comprehensive ban on fishing management system for “one river, one river, one lake.”
