Just saw an article about menstruation, too long.
For males who don't know:
Most girls will experience their first menstrual period sometime between the ages of 13-15 and go through menopause around age 50.
Under the condition of good health, it would come relatively regularly once a month.
But it's not precise enough to be a reliable forecast; about a week's tolerance needs to be prepared in advance, and sometimes bad luck can also lead to issues (with things happening several days earlier than expected).
The duration of time, blood loss, and severity of menstrual cramps vary from person to person; some experience it for three days, while others for a week; some lose very little blood, while others have heavy bleeding; some feel no discomfort, while others suffer so much that they need to be transported by ambulance. Menstrual cramps can change over time; just because it was not painful before does not mean it will never be painful in the future.
Menstruation is uncontrollable, with blood flowing continuously for days from start to end; the only thing one can do is use sanitary napkins or tampons to absorb the blood to prevent it from spilling onto clothes or chairs.
Menstrual cramps are mainly caused by prostaglandin secretion. Taking ibuprofen immediately at the beginning of menstruation can effectively relieve the pain for that month, but taking it after the cramps begin is ineffective.
During the pre-menstrual period, diarrhea can occur due to uterine congestion and excessive release of prostaglandins.
If it happens around the time of her cycle, and for no apparent reason she starts having diarrhea, she will likely get her period within two days.
Sanitary napkins should be changed every two to three hours, otherwise bacteria can easily grow. Excluding sleep time, the worst-case scenario is changing them six times a day for 7 days, which totals 42 pads. If you add in the extra pads prepared in advance, it might be up to 50 pads, not using just one pad per month.
(The modification being made is that sanitary pads do not need to be changed frequently; about every five to six hours is sufficient, with a maximum of eight hours.)
Menstrual hormones can make you moody, and ovulation can also be unstable due to hormonal changes, which is just bad luck as your body is working against you.
But official crime rates show that women still possess high levels of emotional control and do not use emotional issues as an excuse.
What flows is not just blood; it includes detached uterine lining.
The endometrium of the uterus is thinnest after menstruation and gradually thickens again, which can be understood as the uterus being like a house that gets built each month (thickening of the endometrium) to welcome guests. When the guests do not arrive, everything is dismantled (menstrual discharge), and a new house is built every month, repeating this cycle.
Menstruation is very inconvenient, lasting for decades with bleeding for several days every month, accompanied by abdominal pain and backache. Who would want that?
But people are born because their mother has had menstruation. Menstruation is to prepare the house you will live in for ten months when you first come into this world. Everyone should thank their mothers for having regular and healthy periods.
In the past, many things that women did were suppressed, and society brainwashed them into thinking these were dirty and shameful. Everyone thought that was just the way it was, but times have changed.
If anyone finds menstruation repulsive and considers the bodily preparation for conception by women as disgusting and inappropriate to discuss in public, girls should stand up and stop thinking like that. Men who have never experienced it nor will in this lifetime and know very little about it should not be so ungrateful and uncaring. How can one face their mother with such an attitude, or want their child when harboring such thoughts?
Lastly, try out sanitary pads, they really work well.
