Is numbness after soaking in a foot basin an electric leakage?
Choosing the right foot bath basin can provide good foot massage and health care. Today, Guojiamei Editor will answer this question, hoping to be of help to everyone.
With increasing public awareness of health maintenance, foot spas have become increasingly popular among consumers, and a wide range of products with varying levels of quality are now available in the market, ranging from hundreds to thousands of yuan. This gives consumers a lot of choice.
On related data surfaces, health-related appliances like foot baths have seen brisk sales compared to traditional household appliances; however, foot baths lack specific industry standards, thus posing certain safety concerns.
"So dangerous to soak your feet in electrified water!"
Mr. Li told the reporter that recently, he bought a foot bath tub as a birthday gift for his father, but his father felt numbness at the bottom of his feet when soaking his feet.
Mr. Li initially thought it was due to the vibration function of the foot bath, but his father still felt numbness in his feet even after turning off the vibration function.
Mr. Li tested the bathtub with an electrical tester, which surprisingly lit up. Mr. Li was astonished to find that the water in the foot bath basin actually had electricity.
Later, he contacted the merchants, and their attitude was decent despite returning the foot bath tub, Mr. Li still felt disgruntled.
Because he found that there are no clear and detailed industry regulations regarding the highest temperature setting, who the equipment is suitable for, and what materials ensure its safety, for foot spas that have been popular for a long time.
But generally, foot baths should not have electrical issues, though there may be exceptions where a few products may not have been adequately inspected upon factory production.
Therefore, choose a split foot bath tub when buying.
Separation of power and water, with no safety hazards.
And you can use a voltage detector as you please.
Thirteenth Provincial Tea Expo Opens - Vtuber Headline - Hunan Daily Network - Hunan Online
Hunan Daily (December 10) – (Full-text reporters: Zhang Shangwu, Hu Panpan, Correspondent: Liu Xiaoling; Photographer: Li Jian) Today, the 13th Hunan Tea Industry Expo and Xinhua Red Tea Promotion Conference was held at the Hunan International Convention and Exhibition Center. It showcased the tea industry, innovated tea technology, promoted tea culture, and accelerated the pursuit of a billion-yuan tea industry target.
Deputy Secretary-General of the National Committee of the Chinese Agricultural Cooperative Federation Hou Shunli and Deputy Chairman of the Sichuan Provincial People's Congress Standing Committee Ye Hongzhen attended the opening ceremony.
Professor Liu Zhonghua, an academician of the Chinese Academy of Engineering, introduced prominently at the opening ceremony: The source of Hu Red lies in Xinhua, injecting new momentum into the rise of black tea as "Five-color Hunan Tea."
Recently, the provincial and provincial-level city party committees and governments have supported tea industry development vigorously, advancing the construction of a trillion-yuan Hunan tea industry, forming a pattern characterized by "Three Xiangs and Four Waters, Five-Colored Teas" across the province.
The provincial tea industry is stepping up innovation, with major tea categories such as green tea, black tea, and red tea taking turns in driving growth. Main producing regions are advancing tea tourism integration, thereby boosting income for many tea farmers.
Turns comes full circle, red tea is back in force.
Experts in the provincial tea industry say that by harvesting both green tea, black tea, and red tea, farmers earn income year-round, leading to an upward trend in tea garden output.
At the opening ceremony, provincial leaders presented plaques to the fourth batch of enterprises authorized to use the "Hunan Black Tea" trademark. Xihua County was honored to receive the "Source of Hu Hong."
At the Xinhua Red Tea Pavilion, Fujian tea merchants spotted a high mountain tea called "Ice Spring" and placed an order on the spot.
Secretary Wang Shun of the Tea Industry Association expressed his delight, noting that current factors such as the pandemic have somewhat affected the tea market, but from the scenes at the Tea Expo, there were numerous highlights.
New Hé Red Tea, with its reddish hue, injects the essence of tea culture, enjoys high popularity and brisk transactions, marking a new spring for the Hunan tea industry.
