Metaphysics is the study of questions that cannot be directly answered through perception using rational inference and logic.
Metaphysics is the fundamental law of philosophy aimed at explaining the basic nature of existence and the world.
The Chinese term "形而上学" is derived from the phrase "形而上者谓之道，形而下者谓之器" in The易经•系辞上传 of the I Ching, which was translated by the Japanese thinker Kenkichi Ikeno (during the Meiji period) as "metaphysic."
The branches of metaphysics include ontology, natural theology, and related issues.
Ontology is the study of “which nouns represent real existent entities and which nouns merely represent concepts.”
Metaphysical naturalism, which denies the existence of God.
Its theoretical foundation posits that all phenomena can be explained by natural reasons, a concept advanced by philosophers who support science and evolution.
And method naturalism refers to a research method that speaks not a word about God.
Philosophy of mind is the branch of philosophy that studies the essence of consciousness and its relationship with the body (especially the brain).
Dualism and monism are the main schools of thought discussing “the relationship between mind and body.”
Epistemology is a branch of philosophy that explores the nature, origin, and scope of knowledge.
Ethics is a discipline that systematically thinks and studies about human moral life; here, morality refers to all behavioral norms that are recognized by a group of people or a culture.
Mainly divided into meta-ethics (abstractly studying ethical theories or criticizing their essence) and normative ethics (evaluating various different moral views and offering moral guidelines for correct or incorrect behaviors).
It explores what kind of moral behavioral codes people should adhere to), applied ethics (an academic field that applies ethical theories to actual life situations, including bioethics and political ethics), and descriptive ethics (collecting information about how people live, summarizing from observed patterns.)
Its examination of ethics is not from an anticipated theoretical standpoint but from investigating actual moral subjects (moral agents) who make and are observed to make real choices, considering all the moral factors that underlie these decisions, it explores the ethical views held by social groups.
The history of ethics includes Greek and Roman ethics, medieval Christian ethics.
Part 1: Humanist ethical thought, represented by authors such as Valla and Montaigne.
Second Part: The Ethical Thought of the Reformation, Represented by Calvin.
