Ting Zilin: To a Friend.
I sincerely thank all our friends for their humanitarian assistance over the past 30+ years, especially since I issued a call for humanitarian aid both domestically and internationally in 1994 under my personal name, and the continuous support that has never ceased until now.
Nowadays, the elderly parents of most victims have already passed away, and orphans have grown up, married, and entered the workforce.
It was your act of kindness that helped us, these beleaguered families, through our most difficult times.
The current pandemic is raging globally, and most donors are already in retirement age.
Therefore, I earnestly request you to suspend your humanitarian donations to us, the Tiananmen Mothers, as this humanitarian aid effort should have concluded with a perfect closure long ago.
I shall forever remember your kindness and believe that your deeds will be etched into history.
For various reasons, I can only express my wishes in this way, please understand my friends!
Accept my sincere blessings.
Qwen: Tao notes here that, with this opportunity, he would like to provide a brief introduction to the 32-year history of humanitarian aid undertaken by the International Federation of Chinese Scholars (IFCSS) and the Minnesota Chinese Democracy Foundation (MCDF).
During humanitarian aid efforts, the American Association of Universities and the Minnesota China Democracy Foundation are parallel organizations with sometimes overlapping executors.
(Courtesy of Apple Daily: Tiananmen Mother website is managed by Tao Ye.) pic.twitter.com/UxID9ZGjzm
Relief funds often fail to reach the hands of the June 4th victims smoothly.
In 1994, under the suggestion of Teacher Ding, the Tiananmen Mothers established a special group to coordinate and manage the receipt and distribution of humanitarian aid funds.
In 1998, MCDF established the "Orphan Fund for the June 4th Education," which was executed for 11 years before MCDF set up the "Special Assistance Fund for the Hard-pressed June 4th Widows and Orphans" after all the June 4th orphans had graduated from university in 2009, and it has been continuously implemented since then.
The funds for these projects mainly came from donations made on campuses and in society during June Fourth, generous individual contributions, and were supported by the Democratic Foundation of Sister Universities.
Since 2018, it has also received support from the Prison Reform Research Foundation.
It was not until last year that the Six Four victims finally received some of the partial donations collected under the name of June 4th in Hong Kong, over 31 years after the event.
During this time, the "Human Rights in China" also carried out its humanitarian aid projects independently.
We understand that Teacher Ding stopped the donations for June 4th out of consideration for the donors, and we are indeed some retired individuals whose income is not as plentiful as it was before retirement. We hereby express our gratitude for Teacher Ding's letter to his friends.
We will respect the decision of the minorities and terminate this project within two years, and reinstate it immediately when necessary.
To all the brave souls who have long been generous in their contributions, risking it all to send humanitarian aid, we express thanks and respect.
