A programming newbie who wants to share his personal experience.
If you're planning to enter this industry, it might be worth considering finding a place for training; however, if you don't have such an idea, self-study is better, as training can be very expensive and requires six months of study.
But if you're planning to dip your toe into this industry, training is indeed a very good choice, especially for those with very weak self-control, where training is even better.
One can choose to self-study if one has strong self-discipline, though it would be more tiring.
Of course, choosing a good training institution is very important.
The first thing to look at is the faculty strength of the institution. If the faculty strength is not strong enough, then the training institution would not have much value. Of course, some newly established institutions may not have particularly strong faculty strength.
Then, do some research on the training institution before choosing it, preferably by finding out about the students that have come out of it. After all, only the students from the institution would truly know how good or bad it is, and what the courses are really like.
Hello, actually, I'm not sure how to answer this question because I haven't provided an introduction to your specific situation, and learning about this topic is very much related to your own circumstances.
For instance, fields of expertise, age, the language you wish to learn, even in which city you live, can all be related to your learning or desired industry.
If you are a student who has not yet graduated or a computer science graduate, I personally recommend self-study if there is enough time. This is the most efficient and cost-effective approach. Many offline and online training institutions are actually quite good, but the downside is that the learning costs are high, and the investment of time is also significant, usually requiring concentrated training for 3 to 6 months.
But if it were these situations that I am talking about, self-learning would be very convenient. There are many free learning resources on the internet, and there are also plenty of training resources on Bilibili. If you don't know how to find resources, you can contact me via the introduction to get free resources, or you can also consult in detail.
There are also some classic books that can be combined with videos for learning.
If you are not majoring in computer science but can afford the financial and time costs of learning, it is recommended to enroll in a training institution. This way, learning won't be too strenuous, as the teachers explain things very thoroughly, and there will be someone to answer any questions. Additionally, it will provide help with learning planning or supervision.
And many training institutions now sign employment agreements and offer internal referral slots; after completing their studies, they recommend students to companies, which can save a lot of time during the job interview process later on.
That is my take on it. What I mentioned earlier was general and not overly detailed because I didn't know your specific situation. If you have any other questions or topics you'd like to discuss, feel free to contact me at any time for further exchange.
