Yunnan: Coordinated and standardized management of rural public welfare positions - Xinhua News Agency
Xinhua News Agency, Kunming, Dec. 12 (Xinhua) — To continue doing employment assistance for people who have been lifted out of poverty and to consolidate and expand the results of poverty alleviation, Yunnan province recently issued policies on the management of rural public welfare positions to help advance the comprehensive revitalization of rural areas.
Related officials from the Yunnan Provincial People's Office said that at present, Yunnan has developed a total of 44,830,000 rural public benefit jobs to accommodate rural hardworking groups such as the poverty-stricken labour force, which play an important role in helping poverty-stricken people achieve job growth, consolidate the achievements of poverty alleviation, and effectively enhance public service capacities in rural areas.
This new regulation was jointly issued by seven departments including the Yunnan Human Resources and Social Security Bureau, Rural Revitalization Commission, and Finance Department, aiming to unify standards for the scope of individuals hired for village public welfare positions, fund management, position development, and position management at the grassroots level.
Regarding position development settings, the new rules clarify that positions should be set according to the principles of “need-driven positioning, job-based staffing, dynamic management, and total control,” “who develops, who uses, and who is responsible,” as well as “one person, one job, voluntary disclosure, and unified management.”
The recruitment process must follow steps such as announcement, application, review, public display, appointment, pre-employment training, and assignment to duty.
Furthermore, the new regulations clarify the specific responsibilities of the main departments responsible for developing rural public-interest positions, the subsidy standards and methods for these positions, and stipulate that each department responsible for developing rural public-interest positions should purchase commercial insurance for the individuals they have placed in such positions; they also require relevant authorities to regularly oversee the distribution of subsidies for rural public-interest positions.
