In the past two years, the US's blockade and encirclement of Huawei's chips have not only left Huawei in an unprecedented predicament but also resulted in significant losses for "US-based" enterprises.
Meitlin's crackdown on the Chinese chip industry, demanding that its own companies and allied companies simultaneously block China's chip industry, directly resulted in orders and profits being affected by global chip suppliers, resulting in a decrease in R&D and capacity for chip manufacturing companies, resulting in a shortage of chip supply chains worldwide.
Today, "chip shortage" has affected virtually every sector worldwide, from carmakers halting production to rising prices for smartphones, with almost every industry that relies on chips suffering its consequences.
To secure “chip” autonomy, more tech giants from around the world are taking a “de-Americanized” path.
To firmly grasp global semiconductor control and curb the development of China's semiconductor industry, recent U.S. actions have been frequent. This includes hosting a “chip summit”; inviting major CEOs from 64 countries to establish a semiconductor alliance; and allocating funding to support U.S. tech giants. For example, longtime U.S. technology giant Intel, aiming to regain dominance in the chip sector, invested $20 billion to build two semiconductor foundries in Arizona and opened up its chip manufacturing business to the public.
When most thought that Intel’s move would have some impact on TSMC and possibly steal TSMC’s “main dish.”
It is so certain because Intel, as an old giant in American tech, has been deeply involved in the semiconductor field for decades and possesses a large pool of talent and technological reserves; it is bound to rise quickly and defeat TSMC in the short term.
However, Taiwan Semiconductor founder Morris Chang is not impressed with Intel’s decision.
At the "2021 Masters Think Tank Forum" held in April, Tsou Maw-Kuo made a startling statement regarding Intel's investment in building a semiconductor foundry. Tsou bluntly said that Intel's move to build a chip manufacturing plant is quite ironic.
Chan Chung-mou was dismissive of Intel’s foray into wafer manufacturing because he remains resentful of a grudge from 30 years ago when TSMC and Intel had a contentious relationship.
In the 1960s and 1970s, Intel was a shining gem in American manufacturing, occupying the top position globally and the dominant force in the world's semiconductor sector.
By the 1980s, the world's high-end semiconductor chips were largely dominated by a few strong players.
At that time, the semiconductor industry was characterized by the IDM model, where chips were designed, manufactured, and tested by a single factory. Intel was the representative of that era.
However, this model imposes too heavy a burden on small enterprises, and many semiconductor entrepreneurs are deterred from building their own production lines due to huge construction costs.
Driven by market demand, the semiconductor industry began to exhibit phenomena of division of labor and cooperation after entering the 1980s. In 1984, Zhang Zhongmo heard that there were companies specializing in chip design, and he believed that since there was already a company for design, it would be possible to establish a factory specifically for wafer manufacturing.
Not long after, Zhang Zhongmo’s vision became a reality, and TSMC was born.
TSMC focuses its core business on “wafer fabrication” as a single stage, without designing, it solely provides foundry services for wafer manufacturing.
When it was first founded, Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company (TSMC) was just a minor player in the face of Intel, the behemoth.
To secure capital support, Zhang Zhongmu even approached Intel Capital to invest in TSMC, but when Intel heard that TSMC was only involved in the manufacturing segment of the semiconductor industry, it seemed too proud and looked down on TSMC, hence decisively refusing to invest in the company.
Zhang Zhongmu also believed that Intel had looked down upon wafer fabrication services at the time.
Never expected that the giant who once looked down on foundry services for semiconductor manufacturing would now have to start doing foundry themselves.
So, Zhang Zhongmei found it ironic that Intel entered foundry manufacturing.
Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company's (TSMC) market value has already surpassed that of Intel.
Facing Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company’s strong offensive, Intel naturally cannot afford to rest on its laurels either.
