First cases of Omicron infection detected in countries like Mauritius - Xinhua News Agency
Xinhua Beijing, December 12 – Comprehensive reports from Xinhua’s overseas correspondents: The African countries of Mauritius, Cyprus, and Malawi have recently reported their first cases of infections with the Omicron variant of the coronavirus.
Mauritius reported its first two cases of Omicron infection on Monday, marking the country's first such case.
According to Minister of Health Kalesh Jagrutipal, one infected individual was a tourist who departed from South Africa on November 27 and arrived in Mauritius; the other infected individual is a local Mauritian who tested positive for a recent positive test after having contact with an individual who had traveled to South Africa recently and returned.
After being quarantined and treated at the hospital, both infected individuals tested negative on nucleic acid tests and have returned to their respective residences.
The Ministry of Health in Cyprus announced on the 10th that three cases of Omicron infection had been confirmed for the first time in the country.
These three are students who returned from overseas travel at the beginning of this month and as they all had mild symptoms, they did not require hospital treatment.
The Serbian government is stepping up its control efforts to prevent the spread of the Omicron strain.
From December 6 to January 10 of the following year, all passengers aged 12 and above entering the country by air will be required to pay to undergo a nucleic acid test at the airport upon arrival.
The Ministry of Health also announced on the 10th that it will begin vaccinating children aged 5 to 11 in the country with Pfizer COVID-19 vaccines.
Malawi’s Minister of Health, Queen Malizz Chiphonda, said on September 9 that the country has identified its first three cases of Omicron infection.
The national health department conducted genomic sequencing on 12 positive samples collected from November 9 to December 2 and identified these three cases of infection.
