Star Crossings | Twin Scorpion Meteor Shower Reaches Maximum on the 14th - Xinhua News Agency
Xinhua News Agency, Tianjin, December 12 (Xinhua reporter Zhou Runjian) – Experts in astronomical popularization introduced that as one of the last large-scale meteor showers of the year, the Geminids, one of the three major meteor showers in the Northern Hemisphere, will reach its peak on December 14th, and enthusiasts who enjoy meteor showers can observe it after midnight on that day.
Beijing amateur astronomer An Jiu took this image of the Gemini meteor shower on December 13, 2018, in Qinghai.
Most meteor showers have their parent bodies in the form of periodic comets, but the Orionids' parent body is the 3200 Phaethon asteroid, which is an Apollo-type asteroid.
The Gemini meteor shower is also the second known meteor shower that is not "born" from a comet.
Shengli Peng, an astronomer and science popularizer of the Tianjin Astronomical Society, explains that much like a New Year’s blockbuster film, the December conjunction meteor shower appears around the end of the year, with its “release date” lasting from December 4th to the 17th.
This meteor shower is renowned for its high and steady flow, along with the Quadrantid and Perseid meteors, it is known as one of the three major northern hemisphere meteor showers.
One distinctive feature of the Gemini Meteor Shower is that the meteoroids have a relatively slow speed, offer rich colors, and there are many fireballs, making it suitable for beginners to capture both visually and with astrophotography.
Tianjin amateur astronomer Yang Jing captured the Orionid meteor shower on December 13, 2020, in Jiuzhou, Tianjin.
Astronomical forecasts show that the Geminid meteor shower will peak around 15:00 UTC on December 14th, with a ZHR (under ideal observing conditions, when the radiant is located directly overhead, the number of meteors seen per hour) of 150 at the peak.
Gemini Meteor Shower Recommended Index Chart.
Last year, the Gemini meteor shower left a deep impression, but this year's Gemini meteor shower will be dimmed slightly due to moonlight interference.
November 14 marks the date of the twelfth moon in the lunar calendar when the phase is full waning. The bright moonlight will interfere with observations, fortunately, by the late night the moon will set, and at this time, the radiant point of the Gemini meteor shower will be quite high.
Friends who enjoy meteor showers can observe during these few hours after the moon sets in late evening, but be sure to keep warm and protected against the cold. "
How can one observe as many meteors as possible?
It is not recommended to observe in urban areas; one should choose a location with high altitude, low light pollution, facing northeast, and with an open view in suburban or rural areas. Then, patiently wait until the moon sets before visually observing.
Note that the flow of the Gemini meteor shower is very high in all directions, so you don't need to keep your gaze fixed on the radiant point of the Gemini meteor shower when observing.
Photography enthusiasts who want to capture stunning "meteor shower" scenes should ideally choose wide-angle and large-aperture lenses and shoot with high ISO and maximum aperture.
Considering stability and durability, a tripod, a spare battery, and a shutter release cable are also essential.”
Xiu Li Peng reminded.
What are the differences in market positioning between laser TVs and traditional TVs?
In my understanding, the traditional TV that the OP is referring to is likely a LCD TV, and the differences in market positioning between laser TVs and LCD TVs mainly include the following two points:
First, laser TVs target the large-screen market more specifically.
In contrast to the all-encompassing market positioning of LCD TVs, laser TVs are clearly more focused on the large-screen market, or in essence, laser TVs were born for large screens.
Currently, mainstream LCD TVs are typically in the 55-inch to 65-inch range, while laser TVs generally come in sizes of 75 inches or larger, with some models exceeding 100 inches, fully meeting users' demand for big-screen TVs.
In recent years, the shipment and sales share of laser TVs globally have been increasing, with a share exceeding 60% in overall television sales for televisions 80 inches and above.
According to Zhong Yi Kang's forecast, the laser television market sales will grow by more than 140% in 2020 and continue to maintain strong growth.
The excellent performance of laser TVs in terms of eye protection is largely responsible for this good performance in the big screen market.
Based on the imaging principle of laser televisions that use diffuse reflection from lasers, the light emitted by the laser source does not directly enter the eye, and compared to the backlighting of LCD TVs, the imaging process is closer to how objects would naturally be imaged, producing less stimulation to the eye.
The China Electronics Technology Standardization Research Institute's SASTI Laboratory and ophthalmologists from Beijing Tongren Hospital conducted a rigorous comparative test of laser TVs and LCD TVs under the same conditions, resulting in 144 sets of testing data.
Data confirms: Laser TVs indeed have strong eye protection properties, and in certain aspects, they even possess some improving effects.
With increasing emphasis on eye health, laser TVs have attracted an ever-growing number of users not only due to their eye-friendly features but also opened up another avenue in the TV market.
Second, laser TVs target the high-end market more.
Contrary to the continuous decline in LCD TV prices, currently available laser TVs typically cost over ten thousand yuan, with prices gradually increasing as screen sizes and light sources improve.
Why are laser TVs so expensive?
It is certainly reasonable.
With advancements in light sources and technologies, laser TVs have several advantages that LCD TVs and projectors cannot achieve.
With ultra-short throw projection technology, laser TVs have a shorter projection distance compared to traditional projection devices, allowing for a 100-inch screen experience at short distances with minimal space required, and the host is compact, occupying a small area.
In terms of brightness, high-brand laser TVs can reach over 2000 lumens.
Furthermore, with lower heat generation and lesser equipment wear, TV lifespan is also prolonged.
Moreover, many high-end laser TV models come equipped with true 4K ultra-high definition picture quality, artificial intelligence systems, and lifelike screens, among other cutting-edge technologies, which can offer users a more immersive audio-visual experience.
It's not a case of false demand but rather the result of demand segmentation. In terms of picture quality and return on investment, laser TVs aren't suited for audiovisual enthusiasts. If you want to balance big screens with poor viewing environments, you can give it a try. Everything has its pros and cons; weigh the options yourself after considering them, lest you fall prey to one-sided information.
Simply put, ultra-wide angle short-throw projectors – such as laser TVs, which have to use expensive ultra-wide-angle lenses and mirrors to project close to the screen, do not adhere to optimal optical design.
To block some ambient light, using microstructured Fresnel screens is expensive, and an 80-inch model is basically equivalent to a 65-inch LCD screen in price.
One more thing, I really hate how projector manufacturers tout diffuse reflection being good for your eyes as a selling point. Please, the light reflecting off a screen that your eyes can see is essentially direct glare; even if you diffuse it to the ceiling, your eyes won’t see it, and isn’t a regular LCD also considered to have "diffuse direct" lighting? When you turn on the TV, doesn’t the ceiling get illuminated anyway?
And, if the projection screen is not diffuse, it cannot form an image.
The hot spot (glare) effect of high-gain screens, where a large amount of light is reflected at a certain angle and hurts the eyes, has been overlooked.
