Is it reliable for a small umbrella to buy insurance?
Little Umbrella is an internet-based insurance brokerage platform.
Under the conditions stipulated: According to the "Regulations on the Supervision and Administration of Insurance Brokers (Revised in 2015)", to become an insurance broker company, one must meet three key terms: no major illegal records over the past three years for shareholders or initiators, a minimum requirement of RMB 50 million, and must obtain approval from the China Insurance Regulatory Commission (CIRC).
These are quite stringent requirements. In summary, they entail having the capability, being approved by the national authority department—the China Banking and Insurance Regulatory Commission (which oversees all insurance companies and banks in the country)—and having good corporate governance from shareholders. For Xiaoyuxia Insurance, an insurance brokerage company, policyholders don’t have to question whether Xiaoyuxia Insurance is legitimate or not since there are these three thresholds; not every insurance company can meet them.
So far, so good, and trustworthy.
From insurance products standpoint, Xiaoyushan Insurance can be seen as an insurance supermarket where many companies' star critical illness insurance products are sold on this platform, such as the latest HuaTai Super Mary 5th Generation Critical Illness Insurance, with both the claimant and insurer being HuaTai Life Insurance. Xiaoyushan Insurance is merely a third-party intermediary; there's no need to doubt its reliability.
From a payment model standpoint: the products of internet platforms have lower operating costs due to the absence of branch expenses and employee salaries, making them generally more cost-efficient. They also offer flexible payment options, supporting monthly payments, which reduces the financial burden on policyholders. This approach maximizes the leverage of insurance products.
This is still quite good.
It can be considered.
From an insurance professional standpoint: This is particularly important because insurance is a highly professional industry, and many internet product customers can now self-insure. This can lead to numerous misconceptions, especially in health disclosures and occupation categories. Non-insurance professionals often add their subjective opinions when purchasing insurance, believing they are healthy or meet the criteria, which can create potential risks for claims. There is a risk of being denied coverage as a result.
I used to do insurance telephone sales at Tencent Micro Insurance (similar to Xiaoyushuang, an internet insurance brokerage platform), and I feel it is responsible to share this with you.
Internet insurance is real and reliable, but unreliable are people. There are no professional agents in the internet channels, and there is also much misleading sales.
For example: 1 yuan insurance covers 6 million, buy insurance and get a phone, free to receive a 60,000 critical illness coverage. These marketing strategies are not wrong, but they gloss over important details and do not inform the policyholder that the first month is 1 yuan, but the second month and onwards will be charged at normal rates, which can range from several tens of yuan to 200 yuan per month. Many customers are unclear about what they are actually purchasing after deducting fees for a few years.
Thinking you can pay 1 yuan and get compensation of 6 million (a free lunch falling from the sky).
Free phones are given out for special policy numbers like 6666 or 8888 in the policy tail number, and if the winning message is sent but not exchanged by the recipient, no one will remind them.
(This is another trap for policyholders) Free to receive a 60,000 yuan critical illness insurance coverage, it's temporary. If relying solely on this, without other commercial insurance, if a serious illness does occur, what can 60,000 yuan do? It won't solve the problem, and one's future life will start living in poverty.
Currently, the state is also cracking down heavily on misleading sales of internet insurance.
So in summary, the platform is fine and the product is fine; the issue lies with the professionalism and ethical conduct of the sales personnel.
It is recommended that the main topic consider carefully that, in fact, many offline insurance brokerage companies offer a wider range of products, providing customers with more choices and options, and some products have better value for money compared to internet-based products.
I have helped 59 internet clients by underwriting offline products, saving each an average of 2000-3000 yuan per year.
If you have any questions, you are welcome to communicate and communicate with me.
I am Mingya Insurance Broker, with three years of experience in enterprise management training, serving over a thousand corporate users, and collaborating with more than 100 insurance companies. Before purchasing, I tailor insurance products to meet the client's needs. During the purchase process, I help clients compare policies to secure the best underwriting results for them. Afterward, I handle policy organization and custody, ensuring peace of mind for the clients.
Grow with each customer for life.
I hope my response can be of help to you!
According to the definition by insurance regulatory authorities, internet insurance refers to insurance business where policyholders can independently complete all the procedures of an insurance policy after clicking on an insurance link.
Throughout this process, the policyholder does not need to sign, nor does the insurance consultant need to sign; all steps can be completed autonomously by the policyholder.
Although internet insurance applications may lack “ceremony” and even lack printed paper contracts, this does not in any way affect the validity and legal effect of electronic contracts.
This model is somewhat akin to a conveyor belt in telemarketing, but such insurance purchases also come with a fatal flaw: subsequent services may not keep up.
Insurance is typically a long-term contract lasting several decades. Should we seriously consider whether the "agent" we choose, in the long run, will be a "insurance intermediary" who can accompany us and continue to provide services to us many years later?
